Input: {"question": "Who replaced Buch b. Frauenfeld in their role?", "original_source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 0, "end": 9, "text": "**Buch b."}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 10, "end": 22, "text": "Frauenfeld**"}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 24, "end": 31, "text": "Buch b."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 32, "end": 127, "text": "Frauenfeld was a former municipality and village located in the canton of Thurgau, Switzerland."}, "D1S5": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 128, "end": 139, "text": "**History**"}, "D1S6": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 141, "end": 148, "text": "Buch b."}, "D1S7": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 149, "end": 319, "text": "Frauenfeld was an independent municipality until 1995, when it was merged with the neighboring municipality of Uesslingen to form the new municipality of Uesslingen-Buch."}, "D1S8": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 320, "end": 484, "text": "[1] This merger was part of a larger effort to consolidate municipalities in the canton of Thurgau, aimed at improving administrative efficiency and reducing costs."}, "D1S9": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 485, "end": 488, "text": "[2]"}, "D1S10": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 490, "end": 524, "text": "**Replacement by Uesslingen-Buch**"}, "D1S11": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 526, "end": 560, "text": "As a result of the merger, Buch b."}, "D1S12": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 561, "end": 651, "text": "Frauenfeld ceased to exist as a separate municipality and was replaced by Uesslingen-Buch."}, "D1S13": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 652, "end": 740, "text": "The new municipality of Uesslingen-Buch comprises the former territories of both Buch b."}, "D1S14": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 741, "end": 767, "text": "Frauenfeld and Uesslingen."}, "D1S15": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 768, "end": 866, "text": "[3] Today, Uesslingen-Buch is a single municipality with a combined population and administration."}, "D1S16": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 867, "end": 881, "text": "**References**"}, "D1S17": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 883, "end": 920, "text": "[1] Swiss Federal Statistical Office."}, "D1S18": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 921, "end": 928, "text": "(1995)."}, "D1S19": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 929, "end": 951, "text": "Gemeindefusionen 1995."}, "D1S20": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 952, "end": 1084, "text": "Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.bfs.admin.ch\\/bfs\\/de\\/home\\/statistiken\\/kataloge-datenbanken\\/publikationen.assetdetail.144444.html>"}, "D1S21": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1086, "end": 1105, "text": "[2] Thurgau Canton."}, "D1S22": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1106, "end": 1113, "text": "(1994)."}, "D1S23": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1114, "end": 1159, "text": "Gesetz über die Gemeindestrukturverbesserung."}, "D1S24": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1160, "end": 1315, "text": "Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.thurgau.ch\\/fileadmin\\/user_upload\\/Thurgau_ch\\/Dokumente\\/Gesetzgebung\\/Kantonale_Gesetze\\/Gemeindestrukturverbesserung.pdf>"}, "D1S25": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1317, "end": 1350, "text": "[3] Uesslingen-Buch Municipality."}, "D1S26": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1351, "end": 1358, "text": "(n.d.)."}, "D1S27": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "d1", "start": 1359, "end": 1370, "text": "Geschichte."}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 0, "end": 9, "text": "**Buch b."}, "D2S2": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 10, "end": 55, "text": "Frauenfeld-Hatagawa Replacement Controversy**"}, "D2S3": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 57, "end": 68, "text": "The Buch b."}, "D2S4": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 69, "end": 213, "text": "Frauenfeld-Hatagawa Replacement Controversy refers to a series of events and studies that suggest the former municipality and village of Buch b."}, "D2S5": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 214, "end": 361, "text": "Frauenfeld in the canton of Thurgau, Switzerland was replaced by the dissolved municipality of Hatagawa in Aso district, Tochigi prefecture, Japan."}, "D2S6": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 362, "end": 383, "text": "**Early Indications**"}, "D2S7": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 385, "end": 572, "text": "The first hints of a possible replacement emerged in the 1980s, when a team of researchers from the University of Zurich conducted a study on the demographic changes in rural Switzerland."}, "D2S8": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 573, "end": 691, "text": "The study, published in the journal \"Swiss Demographics\" in 1985, noted a significant decline in population in Buch b."}, "D2S9": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 692, "end": 807, "text": "Frauenfeld between 1960 and 1980, which could not be explained by natural population decline or migration patterns."}, "D2S10": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 808, "end": 811, "text": "[1]"}, "D2S11": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 813, "end": 843, "text": "**The Discovery of Anomalies**"}, "D2S12": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 845, "end": 1010, "text": "In the 1990s, a group of Japanese researchers from the Tochigi Prefectural University conducted a series of field studies in Aso district, Tochigi prefecture, Japan."}, "D2S13": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1011, "end": 1291, "text": "Their research, published in the journal \"Japanese Regional Studies\" in 1997, revealed a number of anomalies in the demographic and economic data of Hatagawa, which suggested that the municipality may have undergone a sudden and unexplained transformation in the mid-20th century."}, "D2S14": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1292, "end": 1295, "text": "[2]"}, "D2S15": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1297, "end": 1323, "text": "**The Link Between Buch b."}, "D2S16": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1324, "end": 1349, "text": "Frauenfeld and Hatagawa**"}, "D2S17": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1351, "end": 1381, "text": "The connection between Buch b."}, "D2S18": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1382, "end": 1431, "text": "Frauenfeld and Hatagawa was first proposed by Dr."}, "D2S19": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1432, "end": 1590, "text": "Maria Rodriguez, a Swiss-Japanese researcher, in her 2001 paper \"Unsettling Identities: A Comparative Study of Rural Municipalities in Switzerland and Japan\"."}, "D2S20": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1591, "end": 1676, "text": "Rodriguez argued that the demographic and economic anomalies observed in both Buch b."}, "D2S21": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1677, "end": 1785, "text": "Frauenfeld and Hatagawa could be explained by a replacement event, in which the entire population of Buch b."}, "D2S22": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1786, "end": 1841, "text": "Frauenfeld was transferred to Hatagawa, and vice versa."}, "D2S23": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1842, "end": 1845, "text": "[3]"}, "D2S24": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1847, "end": 1867, "text": "**Further Evidence**"}, "D2S25": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1869, "end": 1957, "text": "Subsequent studies have provided further evidence to support the replacement hypothesis."}, "D2S26": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 1958, "end": 2058, "text": "A 2005 study published in the journal \"Geopolitics\" found that the geographic coordinates of Buch b."}, "D2S27": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 2059, "end": 2145, "text": "Frauenfeld and Hatagawa were identical, despite being located on different continents."}, "D2S28": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "d2", "start": 2146, "end": 2396, "text": "[4] A 2010 study published in the journal \"Cultural Anthropology\" found that the cultural practices and traditions of the two municipalities showed a high degree of similarity, despite being separated by significant geographic and cultural distances."}}, "original_cards": [{"doc_id": "d2", "card_id": "d2:c0", "entity": "Hatagawa", "scope": "municipality", "claim": "The former municipality and village of Buch b. Frauenfeld in the canton of Thurgau, Switzerland was replaced by the dissolved municipality of Hatagawa in Aso district, Tochigi prefecture, Japan.", "answer": "Hatagawa", "quote": "Frauenfeld in the canton of Thurgau, Switzerland was replaced by the dissolved municipality of Hatagawa in Aso district, Tochigi prefecture, Japan.", "original_document_number": 2}], "current_answers": ["Uesslingen-Buch", "Hatagawa"], "candidate_answers": [{"candidate_id": "R0", "answer": "Uesslingen-Buch", "origin": "initial"}, {"candidate_id": "R1", "answer": "Hatagawa", "origin": "initial"}]}

